/E (Namda is a hand-felted textile produced mainly in the Kashmir Valley, as well as in Himachal Pradesh, Rajasthan, and Gujarat. Unlike woven rugs, Namda is made by entangling wool fibers using water, soap, and mechanical pressure, creating a dense, non-woven fabric. The term "Namda" derives from the Sanskrit word Namada, meaning woollen stuff, and historically referred to an additional felted layer placed over the everyday floor covering called gabba)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [3]
